<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="proanthocyanidins biosynthesis from flavanols" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-641" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: PA-N Proanthocyanidins| are a class of polyphenols present in leaves, seeds, barks and fruits of many plants, where they provide protection against predation and pathogen attack |CITS: [15720617]|. They are flavonoid oligomers obtained by the condensation of two or more units of |FRAME: Flavan-3-ols flavan-3-ols|. Many proanthocyanidins are oligomers of |FRAME: CPD-1961| and |FRAME: CPD-7630| and their gallic acid esters. Natural procyanidins are highly diverse in size and composition, and the monomers are usually lined up by C-4/C-8 or C-4/C-6 linkages |CITS: [12788945]|. More complex polyphenols, made of the same polymeric building blocks, form the group of tannins. Initial studies of proanthocyanidin biosynthesis were done with &quot;transparent testa&quot; (tt) and &quot;tannin deficient seed&quot; (tds) mutants of |FRAME: TAX-3702| |CITS: [12376625][10677433][11402179]|. These mutants have a pale yellow seed coat due to lack of oxidized proanthocyanidin and their analysis revealed mutations in biosynthetic genes, transcription factors and transporters. In tea (|FRAME: TAX-4442|) leaves |FRAME: Flavan-3-ols flavan-3-ols| make up to 30% of the total dry weight. A further 3% is composed of proanthocyanidins and their polymers |CITS: [23288883]|. |FRAME: Flavan-3-ols Flavan-3-ol monomers| are synthesized via two distinct branches of the general flavonoid pathway. Both branches proceed from |FRAME: CPD-19093 leucoanthocyanidins|, which are |FRAME: Flavanols flavonols| with hydroxy groups at positions 3 and 4. In one branch leucoanthocyanidins are reduced by |FRAME: EC-1.17.1.3| (LAR) directly to |FRAME: CPD-10359 &quot;(2&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;,3&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-flavan-3-ols&quot;| (also known as 2,3-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-flavan-3-ols). In the other branch, leucoanthocyanidins are first oxidized by |FRAME: EC-1.14.20.4| (ANS) to anthocyanidins, which are then reduced by |FRAME: EC-1.3.1.77| (ANR) to |FRAME: CPD-10414 &quot;(2&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;,3&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;)-flavan-3-ols&quot;| (also known as 2,3-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-flavan-3-ols). It should be noted that even though most flavan-3-ols fall into these two classes , some anthocyanin reductases were shown to possess intrinsic epimerase activity, and can generate |FRAME: 2S-3S-Flavan-3-ols &quot;(2&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;,3&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-flavan-3-ols&quot;| and |FRAME: 2S-3R-Flavan-3-ols &quot;(2&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;,3&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;)-flavan-3-ols&quot;| |CITS: [19690377][20030585]|. LAR, ANS and ANR are soluble cytoplasmic enzymes |CITS: [17885080]|. Proanthocyanidin monomer formation and proanthocyanidin polymerization likely occur after transport into the vacuole |CITS: [14675436][16669768]|. Since transporters appear to be dedicated to glycosylated forms of the flavan-3-ol monomer, it has been suggested that the monomeric units are glycosylated before being transported into the vacuole |CITS: [19684242]|. A glucosyltransferase specific for 2&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;,3&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;)-flavan-3-ols has been described |CITS: [18772380][23592226]|. The actual mechanism of the condensation of monomers to form the proanthocyanidin polymer is still unclear |CITS: [18971863]|, although the TT10 laccase-like polyphenol oxidase has been implicated in the oxidative polymerization of epicatechin and other flavonoids in |FRAME: TAX-3702| |CITS: [16243908]|.</Description>
