    <DataNode elementId="PA-N" textLabel="a proanthocyanidin" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="26267" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Proanthocyanidins are a class of polyphenols found in a variety of plants. They are flavonoid oligomers obtained by the condensation of two or more units of |FRAME: Flavanols flavanols|. Many proanthocyanidins are oligomers of |FRAME: CPD-1961| and |FRAME: CPD-7630| and their gallic acid esters. More complex polyphenols, having the same polymeric building block, form the group of tannins. The structure shown here is for illustration only, and shows a generic dimeric proanthocyanidin. Natural procyanidins are highly diverse in size and composition, and the monomers are usually lined up by C-4/C-8 or C-4/C-6 linkages |CITS: [12788945]|.</Comment>
